    array.push(cH, "US factories wobbled today, yet the dollar's case barely flinched, and Paris had nothing to offer the euro.|Sterling sits at its lowest in a year against the greenback, and today's news did not hand it a rope.|Tokyo kept talking, the Fed kept leaning hawkish, and the pair stays tilted toward the dollar ahead of Japan's CPI.|Wall Street lurched lower and the fear gauge shot above 28 intraday: the franc's comeback trigger is back on.|Carney met Trump in Shenzhen and came back with warm words but no deal, while crude kept leaking lower.|A day after the jobs shock, rate markets dropped every trace of another RBA hike.|The kiwi found some support from its own rate market, but not enough to change the matchup.|Neither side moved on the scoreboard today; the pound's softer tone is still the open question.|Global stocks slid and the yen stayed steady, so this three-point lean keeps hanging by its thread.|The fear trigger that favours the franc lit up again, but this tie needs a score change to break.|The Aussie's rate story turned from 'one more hike?' to 'when is the first cut?', and that eats into this lean.|New Zealand's rate market still expects a hike, which keeps the kiwi a step ahead of a stuck euro.|Canada left Shenzhen empty-handed on the US deal, and the euro waited for Paris: still a coin toss.|The pound drifted and the yen waited for tonight's inflation figure: no lean yet.|Both legs went the wrong way for this lean today: a cautious Bailey and a frightened stock market.|Rate cuts are now priced for Australia, but even that does not create a winner here.|One central bank is still expected to hike, the other just trimmed its 2027 path: the scores stay level.|The loonie missed its US deal again, yet the softer pound keeps this lean under watch.|Risk-off and a dovish RBA repricing on the same day: the carry cross is losing both its supports.|A rate-cut story for the Aussie and a fear spike for the franc: this lean is being squeezed from both ends.|The two rate markets pulled apart today, cuts for Australia and a hike for New Zealand, but the scores have not.|Australia's rate market turned dovish just as Canada's US deal slipped again; the stronger leg is the one cracking.|The kiwi's hike odds and the yen's verbal shield both held: still no lean.|A risk scare is the franc's friend and the kiwi's enemy, and Thursday delivered one intraday.|Canada got no US deal and cheaper oil, while the kiwi kept its hike odds: the lean holds.|Two currencies held by rounding, one with a missed trade deal and one with an inflation test tonight.|The franc's fear trigger fired intraday and the loonie stayed heavy, but the tie survives on paper.|In this haven duel, the franc just got the risk shock its comeback condition describes.|Gold slipped again even as stocks fell, so the haven channel has not helped it yet.")
    array.push(cC, "CONFIRMED (strengthened)~Second-tier data mixed (claims 203k, Philly 6.3) but prices paid jumped to 58.2; hike path intact.|CONFIRMED~No new spread print after 156.3bp (17 Nov); Senate budget debate from Friday.|WEAKENED (watch)~Bailey balanced (no pre-set path); 2027 BoE pricing trimmed to 80bp.|CONFIRMED (watch)~Katayama repeats the warning; December BoJ hike expected; CPI tonight.|INVALIDATED (bullish direction)~VIX 27–28 intraday meets the franc condition (two consistent sources); close pending; score re-measured Friday.|CONFIRMED (bearish reinforced)~Carney after meeting Trump: deal possible but not yet there; oil lower.|INVALIDATED (bearish direction)~Swaps price no RBA hike and a 68% cut by June; shadow +3 central, 0 if R2 is cut.|CONFIRMED~RBNZ 25bp hike priced at 78%; no score change.|CONFIRMED (fragile)~Gold factor −3 unchanged; XAU shadow +3 only via the USD shadow.")
